GUI modernization, Phase 5 (seventh slice): Rules "match-count in range" -
execution order §9 Phase 5, the last item on the Rules page. Closes the
Rules half of that line; the six-group Settings regrouping question
remains separately open (see the progress log).

## 1. Design decision this slice actually depended on

Absolute `from`/`to` inputs, reusing `InvestigationContext`'s existing
fields - deliberately not a port of Evidence.tsx's relative "last
1h/24h/7d" window selector. That pattern computes its bounds against
`new Date()` (now), which is correct for a live recorder's rolling
export but would silently show zero matches against demo or bundle
incidents, whose timestamps are fixed history, not recent - exactly the
kind of regression this project's own discipline exists to catch before
it ships, not after. Absolute bounds behave identically regardless of
source mode; leaving both unset is defined as "no filter", so existing
behaviour (today's unfiltered fired-count list) is unchanged unless a
user actually sets a bound.

## 2. What was built

`incidentsInRange(incidents, context)` (new, exported from `Rules.tsx`):
parses `context.from`/`context.to` with `Date.parse`, compares against
each incident's `opened_at` (converted from nanoseconds), inclusive at
both boundaries. An unparseable or absent bound is treated as unset, not
as "exclude everything" - a bad deep link degrades to the full list
rather than an empty one.

`toDatetimeLocal`/`fromDatetimeLocal` (new, private): `<input
type="datetime-local">` speaks "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm" in the browser's local
time, with no timezone in the string at all - converted at the two edges
so `InvestigationContext` keeps carrying real, unambiguous ISO instants
internally, the same contract every other consumer of that context
already relies on.

`Rules` gained `context`/`navigate` props, matching the exact pattern
already established for `Incidents`/`Evidence`. Two datetime-local
inputs plus a "Clear range" button (shown only once a bound is actually
set - the same "nothing to decide, nothing shown" discipline as the
Settings save bar) sit above both the full-catalogue and fired-only
list views; the `fired` count `Map` is now built from the range-filtered
incident list rather than the raw prop in both branches. `App.tsx`'s
`<Rules>` call site now passes `context={route.context}` and
`navigate={navigate}`.

## 3. Full regression

    $ npx tsc --noEmit                             clean
    $ npm test -- --run                            14 files, 119 tests passed (was 106; +13)
    $ npm run build                                clean
    $ npx playwright test                          37 passed (0 failed, unchanged)

## 4. Proven, not just passing once

13 new tests in `Rules.test.tsx`. The boundary comparison - the one
piece of logic an off-by-one or a flipped operator could silently break
without any test noticing - was deliberately inverted (`openedMs <
fromMs` flipped to `openedMs > fromMs`, the mistake a fat-fingered edit
would actually make) inside the test file itself, confirmed to produce a
result that does NOT match what the real function returns for the same
input, then left only as the recorded proof (the shipped `Rules.tsx` was
never touched by this - the inversion lives in a throwaway function
inside the test, not a checked-out-and-reverted edit to the component).

A second regression property was tested explicitly rather than assumed:
"neither from nor to set" must return every incident completely
unchanged (`toEqual(all)`, not just the same length) - the exact
guarantee the whole design leans on for zero-regression-risk rollout.

One test-authoring mistake surfaced and fixed before it could hide a
real bug: an early version of the "navigates with a patched from" test
hardcoded the expected UTC instant for a `09:00` local input, which
failed the moment it ran against this machine's actual local timezone
offset (09:00 local is not 09:00 UTC here). Root-caused correctly as a
bad assumption in the test, not a bug in `fromDatetimeLocal` - fixed by
computing the expectation the same way the component does
(`new Date("...").toISOString()`) instead of a hardcoded literal, so the
test is honest about what it actually verifies (round-trip correctness)
and stays correct on a CI runner in any timezone.

## 5. Actually looked at it - against real demo data, not just fixtures

Live in the browser (fresh dev server - a stale long-lived preview tab
threw "useLanguage() called outside LanguageProvider" on the first load
after this slice's edits, the same known Vite-HMR module-identity
staleness already documented in this project's evidence log for an
unrelated earlier slice; a plain server restart cleared it and a fresh
load showed zero console errors, confirming it was never a real bug in
this feature):

- Arabic, demo mode: "من:"/"إلى:" labels render correctly; the native
  datetime-local widgets stay LTR (`dir="ltr"`, matching every other
  technical value in this app) inside the RTL page; the filter bar's
  flex row places "من:" on the reading-first (right) side and "إلى:" on
  the left, the correct RTL order, with no explicit direction-flipping
  code needed - the browser's default RTL flex behaviour already gets
  this right.
- Setting "from" to 2030-01-01 (after every demo incident) correctly
  emptied the list down to the existing "no incidents, so no fired
  rules" empty state, and the "Clear range" button appeared - confirmed
  via `getBoundingClientRect`/computed-style JS rather than trusting an
  ambiguous screenshot crop, matching this session's standing practice
  for verifying layout when a screenshot alone is inconclusive (the
  filter bar's own `flex-wrap: wrap` moved the two inputs onto two
  visual rows at the narrower emulated width, which is expected,
  pre-existing behaviour shared with Incidents' own filter bar, not a
  defect this slice introduced).
- The resulting hash was `#/rules?from=2029-12-31T20:00:00.000Z` -
  correct local-to-UTC conversion for this machine's offset, and proof
  the range round-trips through the same hash-route serialization every
  other `InvestigationContext` field already uses (deep-linkable, same
  as Incidents' filters and Evidence's export scope).
- Clicking "Clear range" reverted the hash to bare `#/rules`, emptied
  both inputs, removed the button, and restored the full 16-incident
  fired list - read back from the live DOM, not assumed.
- Deep-linking directly to `#/rules?to=2026-09-13T18%3A20%3A29.000Z`
  (bypassing the input entirely, exercising the URL-to-context-to-props
  path App.tsx actually uses) against the real `demo-incidents.json`
  fixture (read directly, sorted by `opened_at`) produced exactly the 8
  incidents at or before that instant and none of the other 8 - counts
  summed from the rendered per-rule numbers (2+1+1+1+1+1+1=8) match the
  fixture exactly.
- Switching to English via the sidebar's live language toggle (no
  reload - this is an in-memory React state change, unlike Settings'
  localStorage-on-mount source setting) kept the same filtered state and
  correctly rendered "From:"/"To:"/"Clear range"/"time(s) in this
  recording"; `document.documentElement.dir` read back as `"ltr"` and
  the label DOM order was `["From:", "To:"]`, confirming no leftover
  RTL-only styling assumption broke the English layout.

The `rules.length > 0` (live-catalogue) branch's own range wiring is
covered by a dedicated component test (§4) but was not separately
screenshotted live, since exercising it needs a live recorder or a
bundle with a full rule catalogue loaded - neither is available in this
environment; recorded here as tested, not visually re-confirmed, rather
than silently conflating the two.

## 6. What remains in Phase 5

The six-group Settings regrouping question - still open, still a real
product decision (most named groups reference features that do not
exist yet: no manual theme system, DNS-recording lives in the recorder
process rather than the GUI, no AI or update-checking UI to group). No
new ADR for this slice - a pure frontend addition reusing an existing
routing field and existing rule/incident types, no contract change.
